window.EDITORIAL = {"id":"ed_2026-10-20","publishDate":"2026-10-20","status":"published","title":"Beyond the Roast: The Architecture of Modern Craft","dek":"In an evolving industry, precision and collaboration redefine specialty coffee.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/project-iii-release-2-box-copy.jpg","heroCredit":"Phil & Sebastian Announces Second Edition Of Project III, Featuring Ratibor Hartmann, Proud Mary, And MAME","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/phil-sebastian-announces-second-edition-of-project-iii-featuring-ratibor-hartmann-proud-mary-and-mame-1486270.html","wordCount":279,"paragraphs":["The modern coffee landscape continues to shift, moving beyond simple origin stories toward a deeper appreciation for processing, preparation, and international exchange. Today’s headlines highlight how technical rigour, shared expertise, and intelligent equipment design are quietly reshaping what ends up in our cup.","Take collaborative roaster-producer projects like Calgary-based Phil & Sebastian’s second edition of Project III. By placing rare Panamanian Geishas grown by legendary producer Ratibor Hartmann into the hands of three distinct global roasters—Phil & Sebastian, Proud Mary, and Switzerland’s MAME—the project acts as a controlled experiment in flavor development. It demonstrates that roast profiling is not merely a manufacturing step, but a nuanced translation of agricultural craft.","Equipment continues to catch up with this demand for precision. Reviews of Timemore’s S5 hand grinder showcase how hardware engineering is leaning into punchy, high-acidity extractions without sacrificing body, giving light roasts the structural clarity they demand. Meanwhile, annual analytics from Sprudge’s 2026 Build-Outs of Coffee reveal a retail landscape increasingly designed around streamlined workflows, modular bar setups, and intentional spatial ergonomics. Cafés are no longer built solely for speed; they are built to showcase liquid quality while managing the realities of modern operational overhead.","Underpinning these retail and home brewing milestones is a renewed focus on fundamental chemistry and trade globalism. As the Specialty Coffee Association delves into the molecular chemistry of modern decaffeination, global trade gatherings like the TriestEspresso Expo in Italy remind us that the entire value chain—from green buyers and equipment manufacturers to green technology innovators—must remain interconnected. Whether dissecting the extraction profile of a conical burr or exploring multi-roaster interpretations of a single micro-lot, specialty coffee’s fourth wave thrives where curiosity meets uncompromising execution."],"body":"The modern coffee landscape continues to shift, moving beyond simple origin stories toward a deeper appreciation for processing, preparation, and international exchange. Today’s headlines highlight how technical rigour, shared expertise, and intelligent equipment design are quietly reshaping what ends up in our cup.\n\nTake collaborative roaster-producer projects like Calgary-based Phil & Sebastian’s second edition of Project III. By placing rare Panamanian Geishas grown by legendary producer Ratibor Hartmann into the hands of three distinct global roasters—Phil & Sebastian, Proud Mary, and Switzerland’s MAME—the project acts as a controlled experiment in flavor development. It demonstrates that roast profiling is not merely a manufacturing step, but a nuanced translation of agricultural craft.\n\nEquipment continues to catch up with this demand for precision. Reviews of Timemore’s S5 hand grinder showcase how hardware engineering is leaning into punchy, high-acidity extractions without sacrificing body, giving light roasts the structural clarity they demand. Meanwhile, annual analytics from Sprudge’s 2026 Build-Outs of Coffee reveal a retail landscape increasingly designed around streamlined workflows, modular bar setups, and intentional spatial ergonomics. Cafés are no longer built solely for speed; they are built to showcase liquid quality while managing the realities of modern operational overhead.\n\nUnderpinning these retail and home brewing milestones is a renewed focus on fundamental chemistry and trade globalism. As the Specialty Coffee Association delves into the molecular chemistry of modern decaffeination, global trade gatherings like the TriestEspresso Expo in Italy remind us that the entire value chain—from green buyers and equipment manufacturers to green technology innovators—must remain interconnected. Whether dissecting the extraction profile of a conical burr or exploring multi-roaster interpretations of a single micro-lot, specialty coffee’s fourth wave thrives where curiosity meets uncompromising execution."};
window.EDITORIAL_HISTORY = [{"id":"ed_2026-10-19","publishDate":"2026-10-19","status":"published","title":"From Fermentation to Form: The Next Phase of Coffee Innovation","dek":"How climate science, circular chemistry, and creative storytelling are reshaping the specialty landscape from origin to cup.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://coffeetalk.com/wp-content/uploads/susan-wilkinson-xuGGytlK0-E-unsplash-scaled.jpg","heroCredit":"Coffee Talk","heroSource":"Coffee Talk","heroSourceUrl":"https://coffeetalk.com/daily-dose/top-news/10-2026/111161/","wordCount":276,"paragraphs":["The specialty coffee ecosystem is undergoing a dramatic realignment, driven by climate pressures, technological evolution, and shifting consumer habits. Across origin countries, research labs, and roasteries, industry pioneers are finding novel ways to optimize every link of the value chain.","At origin, adaptation is taking both scientific and agricultural forms. A new international trial published in Nature highlights critical genotype-by-environment interactions in Arabica coffee across changing climate gradients, offering vital data for long-term farm resilience. Meanwhile, regional investment is accelerating: the University of Hawaiʻi is set to receive $40 million to support local coffee and cacao producers, strengthening domestic yields. On the marketing front, essential COSTA RICA is tapping into global culture, launching an anime-inspired campaign designed to captivate Asian markets and broaden the appeal of Costa Rican specialty origins.","In the lab, circular sustainability is moving beyond mere buzzwords. Ethiopian researchers have successfully pioneered an ultrasound-assisted extraction method that converts coffee parchment waste into high-purity cellulose, transforming a ubiquitous byproduct into valuable industrial raw material. This spirit of technical progress extends to roasting equipment, where Mill City Roasters has delivered its new M-Series 20kg roaster from its Minneapolis facility—marking its third major hardware release this year as commercial capacity demands grow.","On the retail side, consumer tastes are evolving rapidly. Cold coffee continues its European expansion, highlighted by a 39% spike in UK iced coffee sales, prompting brands to experiment with functional additives and complex flavor profiles. This cultural momentum is anchored by the leaders shaping it, including Oxo’s Chris Diskin, featured in the latest Sprudge Twenty series, alongside groundbreaking research mapping the shared microbial and fermented lineage linking coffee, yogurt, and chocolate across Europe."]},{"id":"ed_2026-10-18","publishDate":"2026-10-18","status":"published","title":"Beyond the Bean: Labor, Climate, and the New Cafe Economics","dek":"As shifting weather patterns disrupt global markets, the specialty coffee industry is adapting through historic union protections, accessible roasting tech, and radical sustainability.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://coffeetalk.com/wp-content/uploads/iStock-528280178.jpg","heroCredit":"Verve Coffee Workers Vote to Ratify Landmark Union Agreement with Industry-First Protections","heroSource":"Coffee Talk","heroSourceUrl":"https://coffeetalk.com/daily-dose/for-roasters-retailers/10-2026/111146/","wordCount":306,"paragraphs":["The specialty coffee industry stands at a fascinating crossroads of climate reality, technological disruption, and labor empowerment. As El Niño weather patterns predictably reshape global coffee markets and threaten crop yields, the pressure on the supply chain has rarely been higher. Yet, the industry is responding not with retreat, but with remarkable resilience.","At the retail level, cafe economics are actively shifting. Bellwether’s debut of its new Shop Roaster offers a compelling lifeline to independent operators, with new calculator data suggesting cafes can effectively cut their coffee costs in half by bringing roasting in-house. This democratization of the roasting process promises both financial stability and enhanced branding for local shops.","But as technology rapidly advances, so does the urgent need to protect the human hands that craft our daily brew. In a landmark victory for labor, Verve Coffee workers recently ratified a historic union agreement. Notably, this contract secures industry-first protections addressing AI management concerns, alongside essential scheduling and sick leave guarantees. It is a powerful reminder that while algorithms and automation have their place, the soul of coffee remains profoundly human.","We see that humanity celebrated in cafes across the globe every day. It lives in the quiet dedication of baristas like Driel Telles de Menezes shaping Lisbon's vibrant scene at Sense of Coffee and The Coffee Bloc, behind the welcoming doors of Prague’s new Tao Coffee, and through the industry-wide recognition of trailblazers like Sprudge Twenty inductee Daniel Borrás.","Even our waste is finding visionary new life, with discarded coffee silverskin now showing distinct promise as a shield against industrial steel corrosion. As a recent Sprudge feature wryly asks: A coat and a nice cup of coffee, is that really too much to ask for? Looking at this week's convergence of fair labor, accessible technology, and ecological ingenuity, the answer is a resounding no."]},{"id":"ed_2026-10-17","publishDate":"2026-10-17","status":"published","title":"Redefining the Coffee Horizon","dek":"From unexpected European terroirs to shifting consumer tastes, specialty coffee is redrawing its global boundaries.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/new_diner_mug_matte_black_white_gloss_gray_b2b_lifestyle_motofish_021226_009.jpg","heroCredit":"Sprudge","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/coffee-sprudgecast-the-one-with-pumpkin-spice-caskets-1416443.html","wordCount":236,"paragraphs":["The boundaries of the coffee world are shifting in real time. Climate change is forcing an unprecedented realignment of agricultural geography, evidenced by Sicilian farmers successfully growing coffee outdoors in Palermo. What once felt like a speculative curiosity is now a tangible signal that coffee’s traditional bean belt is expanding northward into European soil.","This spirit of adaptation extends directly into consumer culture. Specialty coffee is shedding its long-held dogmas, embracing quality wherever it appears—whether in high-end specialty decafs that finally earn a spot on serious brew bars, or in hyper-local European micro-roasters in Ibiza and Norway establishing distinct craft identities. The industry's evolution is no longer defined by strict exclusivity, but by expanding access without sacrificing standard.","At the same time, commercial scale and specialty craft are increasingly crossing paths. Celebrity-driven ventures like Sofía Vergara’s ¡Dios Mío! Coffee launching nationwide in Costco demonstrate how origin storytelling and Colombian beans are securing mass-market shelf space. Meanwhile, corporate trend forecasting points toward a future rich in unexpected flavor profiles, pointing to broader culinary overlaps—where even dental material testing in material science must evaluate how our daily brews alter surface roughness and color stability.","Whether through agricultural necessity, craft refinement, or mass retail expansion, coffee’s modern landscape is being continuously redrawn. As terroirs migrate and consumer expectations diversify, the future of coffee belongs to those willing to adapt to a changing climate and a more dynamic global palate."]},{"id":"ed_2026-10-15","publishDate":"2026-10-15","status":"published","title":"Economics and Physiology in the Modern Cup","dek":"From in-house roasting cost-cuts to startling medical revelations, exploring specialty coffee’s shifting landscape.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/bellwether.jpg","heroCredit":"Sprudge","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/bellwether-debuts-new-shop-roaster-as-data-shows-cafes-can-cut-coffee-costs-in-half-by-roasting-in-house-1457452.html","wordCount":294,"paragraphs":["The specialty coffee industry is currently navigating a complex intersection of tightening economics and evolving scientific understanding. For café operators, the math of survival is being radically rewritten. With wholesale roasted specialty coffee prices flirting with $15 a pound, reliance on legacy distribution models is beginning to wane. Enter technological interventions like Bellwether’s new Shop Roaster, which early data suggests can cut a café’s coffee costs in half, saving owners an estimated $2,000 monthly by bringing production in-house.","This drive toward greater control and vertical integration is echoing across the industry. In Portland, Pastime Coffee is going all-in on a new production roastery, scaling their operations to meet localized demand while maintaining stringent quality oversight. Meanwhile, conversations with producers and innovators—like Segara Coffee's Esther Singer—remind us that sourcing and sustainability remain the bedrock of the trade, even as consumer-facing costs fluctuate.","On the consumer side, science is painting a nuanced portrait of coffee’s physiological impacts. A recent Norwegian study offers promising news for adult consumers, linking coffee consumption to a reduction in repeat blood clots. However, the picture is far less rosy for younger demographics; emerging research correlates children's caffeine habits with shorter sleep durations and concerning brain differences, highlighting the need for mindful consumption in an increasingly caffeinated world.","Yet, the pursuit of the perfect, accessible cup remains undeterred. Coffee Review recently highlighted the \"sweet spot\" of the market, proving that exceptional coffees can still be found for under $20. And the global map of specialty coffee continues to expand into exciting new territories. Whether you are exploring the meticulously crafted menus at Tc Space or navigating the burgeoning café culture of Ružomberok, the dedication to origin and craft proves that the fourth wave of coffee is as resilient as it is dynamic."]},{"id":"ed_2026-10-13","publishDate":"2026-10-13","status":"published","title":"Craft, Convergence, and the Mindful Cup","dek":"Reflecting on the evolving intersections of specialty coffee, gut science, and global culture.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://europeancoffeetrip.com/wp-content/uploads/city-guides/cafes/35750/hetnoordeinde_1791381796_4002723059445851756_2139683154.jpg","heroCredit":"from: Noordeinde","heroSource":"European Coffee Trip","heroSourceUrl":"https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMibkFVX3lxTE90Sjh0NTVYOVRTOUpRRkNYTEtWdEk0clNvdlRZVWJOQVNRY2F3b1cyZHZDdTByd3RDNjVjUENLQVJDMVVrZFNZLXlkMEZTa0ZzSXBoZmdjMVV5eG1UQldEWVpYYmVsYnA4MFQ4bVhn","wordCount":266,"paragraphs":["The modern coffee landscape is undergoing a deliberate, multidimensional shift—one where heritage craftsmanship, emerging health sciences, and institutional momentum seamlessly merge.","Nowhere is this evolution clearer than in our understanding of what happens after the final sip. Recent scientific insights emphasize how extraction methods—from pressure-driven espresso to paper-filtered pour-overs—fundamentally alter the bioactive compounds that reach our gut and brain, revealing how brewing mechanics shape systemic metabolism. Meanwhile, roasted profile precision continues to reach new heights, exemplified by Roast magazine unveiling its 2027 Roaster of the Year finalists and specialty roasters crafting decaffeinated options so refined they rival their caffeinated counterparts.","At the same time, regional identity and global infrastructure are establishing stronger roots. In Japan, Fukuoka City has solidified its standing as a world-class coffee destination, offering a vibrant ecosystem where traditional kissaten heritage meets vanguard extraction standards. Across the globe, institutional trade is gearing up for major gatherings: the National Coffee Association (NCA) announced its return to Austin in March 2027, bringing together key industry players for networking and community-focused initiatives. On an international diplomatic level, Italy’s Foreign Ministry hosted the launch of the Global Coffee Initiative’s Espresso Fund alongside leaders like Andrea Illy and Giuseppe Lavazza, directing crucial investment toward sustainable farming, origin resilience, and the long-term economic stability of smallholder producers.","From local European coffee stops like Noordeinde to high-level diplomatic forums in Rome, today’s industry news reflects a single, unifying truth: specialty coffee is no longer just a morning ritual. It is a sophisticated, global culture rooted in rigorous science, mindful preparation, and sustainable investment across every stage of the supply chain."]},{"id":"ed_2026-10-11","publishDate":"2026-10-11","status":"published","title":"Beyond the Brew Path: Economics, Equity, and Engineering","dek":"As Arabica prices cross a historic threshold, the specialty coffee industry finds crucial innovation not just in the cup, but in how we breed, build, and belong.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/phil-seb-hartmann-maragogype-natural-sprudge-roasters-village-copy.jpg","heroCredit":"Sprudge","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/look-at-all-these-coffees-dont-look-at-them-drink-them-1417089.html","wordCount":354,"paragraphs":["The coffee industry has always balanced on a razor's edge between agricultural tradition and turbulent change, but this week's developments underscore a sector evolving with unprecedented velocity.","Let’s start at the market floor: Arabica prices have surged past the US$3/lb threshold for the first time since early September, propelled largely by a strengthening Brazilian real against the US dollar. Yet, even as wholesale bean prices spike, the retail playbook is rapidly shifting. Costa Coffee's recent return to profitability wasn't fueled by classic espresso offerings, but by the booming consumer demand for matcha and iced beverages.","To survive these dual economic and climate pressures, the industry is rethinking the seed itself. A sweeping international multilocation trial recently published in Nature offers a vital roadmap, revealing exactly how specific Arabica genotypes interact with diverse climate gradients. The data confirms that certain F1 hybrids and introgressed lines provide superior combined performance for yield and rust resistance, offering producers a much-needed buffer against thermal instability.","Innovation is equally vigorous at the consumer level. Home baristas are increasingly treating their morning routines like laboratory work. Whether it’s dialing in extraction with the latest precision espresso scales, or brewing on Caraway's striking new $495 coffee maker—which notably bans all plastic from its internal brew path for a cleaner extraction—the pursuit of purity is paramount. Even the chemistry of our milk drinks is under the microscope. Further research in Nature highlights how the structural reorganization of the milk matrix physically alters oral friction and sensory perception in coffee-milk systems. You aren't just tasting the roast; your palate is actively perceiving the matrix.","But specialty coffee’s truest value remains its human element. You can curate the finest weekly selections from the Sprudge Roaster's Village or analyze profiles in the RoastFinder Coffee Lab, but the soul of the café lives behind the bar. Look no further than Michaela Randell at England’s Ground Up Project, a café initiative providing meaningful hospitality work opportunities for young neurodiverse individuals. As the commodity market fluctuates and the science deepens, it’s community-driven spaces like these that remind us why we care about the cup in the first place."]},{"id":"ed_2026-10-10","publishDate":"2026-10-10","status":"published","title":"Rituals and Realities: From Futures Markets to the Pour-Over","dek":"As global coffee futures fluctuate on corporate sale rumors, the industry continues its push into high-end aesthetics, while new research complicates our understanding of caffeine and health","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/dwell-coffee-gear.jpg","heroCredit":"Sprudge","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/feast-your-eyes-on-this-cornucopia-of-coffee-gear-1417120.html","wordCount":278,"paragraphs":["Specialty coffee has always existed in a strange duality: it is simultaneously a volatile global commodity and an intimately personal morning ritual.","This week, that contrast is sharper than ever. On the macroeconomic front, coffee futures stumbled backward from a Tuesday rally, driven down by resurfacing speculation regarding a potential sale of Costa. Yet, if you look at the consumer side of the industry, you would never know the market was jittery. Premium gear is more accessible—and fashionable—than ever. Costco is currently moving high-end Fellow pour-over essentials at a steal, proving that specialty brewing has fully breached the mainstream retail barrier. Meanwhile, La Marzocco has partnered with Danish outerwear brand Rains to release a highly technical, waterproof barista apron, signaling that coffee workwear is now firmly entrenched in fashion territory.","But what exactly is that daily cup doing to us? The science remains complex. A new study in Nature highlights the double-edged sword of our modern lifestyle, revealing that habitual coffee consumption combined with sleep deprivation alters specific microRNA (miR-30c and miR-127) expressions in adult men, leading to metabolic and inflammatory dysregulation. On the other end of the demographic spectrum, ScienceAlert reports that the choice between tea and coffee can actively influence the risk of osteoporosis and bone density in older women.","Despite the metabolic warnings and market fluctuations, the romance of the ritual remains unbroken. Whether it is finding inspiration in a quiet corner of Cafés Sable in Europe, ogling Sprudge’s latest cornucopia of gear drops, or simply pairing a slow morning brew with one of Serious Eats' cozy fall baking recipes, coffee remains exactly what we need it to be: an anchor in an unpredictable world."]},{"id":"ed_2026-10-08","publishDate":"2026-10-08","status":"published","title":"The Filter: From Compostable Promises to Combat Sports","dek":"Sustainability hits a corporate snag, athletes get a caffeinated edge, and the specialty community honors its ubiquitous connectors.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://coffeechronicler.b-cdn.net/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/americano-perfect-scaled.jpg","heroCredit":"The Best Coffee Machine for Americano? Here Are My Picks","heroSource":"Coffee Chronicler","heroSourceUrl":"https://coffeechronicler.com/best-coffee-machine-for-americano/","wordCount":289,"paragraphs":["Big coffee often promises the moon, but earthly realities tend to get in the way. Case in point: Nespresso recently pulled the plug on its highly touted compostable capsules, ultimately citing concerns over cup quality. It is a stark reminder that massive scale and environmental sustainability remain uneasy bedfellows. Fortunately, the true pulse of the coffee industry beats far away from corporate boardrooms. This week, we are celebrating the individuals who actively knit the global specialty network together. That includes new Sprudge Twenty inductee Esteban Cristancho of Elevation Coffee Roasters, alongside figures like Amalie Gagné-Acoulon. Amalie's omnipresent work—spanning origin farms, global competitions, and language teaching—proves that human connection is still our most vital infrastructure.","Beyond the farm and the cafe floor, coffee continues to serve as a fascinating lens for human behavior. A new study published in Nature backs up what many fighters already suspected: low-dose caffeinated coffee measurably boosts energy systems and repeated sprint indices in combat athletes. Yet, while a quick brew might improve physical output, it can also expose our psychological blind spots. The Conversation recently unpacked how the viral \"iced coffee test\" during job interviews serves no actual purpose in predicting candidate performance, acting instead as a glaring indicator of hiring bias.","On the consumer front, utility is getting a stylish upgrade. While everyday deal-hunters navigate the predictable chaos of Prime Day espresso discounts and gear critics debate the exact hardware required for the perfect Americano, La Marzocco has teamed up with Danish rainwear brand Rains. Their resulting \"coffee-proof\" apron seamlessly marries Scandinavian minimalism with Italian espresso heritage. Whether we are chasing peak athletic performance, fighting workplace bias, or just trying to keep our shirts clean on bar, coffee remains our favorite daily testing ground."]},{"id":"ed_2026-10-07","publishDate":"2026-10-07","status":"published","title":"Shrinking Footprints and Expanding Horizons","dek":"As economic pressures shrink the traditional cafe, the coffee industry is finding new life in functional science and the home-brewing boom.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://coffeetalk.com/wp-content/uploads/viktor-talashuk-HZ3DjriWmIA-unsplash-scaled.jpg","heroCredit":"Coffee Talk","heroSource":"Coffee Talk","heroSourceUrl":"https://coffeetalk.com/daily-dose/top-news/10-2026/111096/","wordCount":301,"paragraphs":["The specialty coffee landscape is currently defined by a striking tension between constriction and expansion. On the physical and economic fronts, the industry is feeling the squeeze. Starbucks recently unveiled a significantly smaller café prototype, a direct adaptation to the punishing realities of modern urban real estate. This spatial contraction parallels a broader economic anxiety: as new energy price caps take effect, the energy-intensive processes of commercial roasting and running heavy cafe machinery threaten to push the cost of a daily cup even higher.","Yet, as the traditional cafe footprint contracts, our conceptual understanding of coffee continues to expand into fascinating new territories. Sociologically, researchers are examining how something as seemingly innocuous as holding an iced coffee can trigger subconscious hiring biases in job interviews, proving our beverage choices carry immense, unstated cultural weight. On a molecular level, the boundaries of the drink are being completely reimagined. Food scientists have successfully linked coffee-derived chlorogenic acids to milk peptides using natural apple juice enzymes, a remarkable breakthrough that could transform coffee from a morning stimulant into a cornerstone of functional food design.","Meanwhile, the combination of rising retail prices and technological democratization is cementing the home barista's power. Massive sales events are moving premium gear at unprecedented rates, from reliable, leakproof Owala tumblers to heavy discounts on Breville’s most popular home espresso machines. Consumers are aggressively taking cafe-level quality into their own hands. Crucially, this home-brewed independence hasn't diluted the demand for top-tier sourcing. Newsweek’s recent recognition of Klatch Coffee as the ‘Best Premium Coffee Brand 2026’ proves that uncompromising specialty standards have fully permeated the mainstream. Coupled with the rising profile of community-focused European outfits like Mutual Coffee, it is clear that no matter where the coffee is ultimately extracted, the demand for exceptional, relationship-driven origin remains the industry's true north."]},{"id":"ed_2026-10-06","publishDate":"2026-10-06","status":"published","title":"The Weight of Legacy: Pushing Boundaries and Preserving Community","dek":"From the relentless pursuit of portable torque to the sobering realities of those we miss, the coffee industry balances high-minded innovation with deeply human roots.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/illustration_luciano-repetto.webp","heroCredit":"Sprudge","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/luciano-repetto-of-graffeo-coffee-the-sprudge-twenty-interview-1278697.html","wordCount":293,"paragraphs":["\"I always want people to remember me after I pass away,\" reflects Ibrahim H. Al Mallouhi, echoing a sentiment that quietly drives much of the specialty coffee world. We are an industry obsessed with what we build and what we serve. That focus on legacy was celebrated this week with Luciano Repetto’s induction into the Sprudge Twenty, honoring his long-standing impact at Graffeo Coffee. Yet, our community’s tight-knit nature also means we feel absences acutely; the somber reality that Farley’s Coffeehouse co-owner Amy Hillyard remains missing six months later is a heavy reminder of the fragile, deeply human core of our daily rituals.","While we honor our people, we remain restless in our pursuit of mechanical and sensory perfection. The quest for the ultimate extraction continues to crash against physical realities. Hardware developers are currently wrestling with the cordless espresso grinder conundrum—proving that sustained, battery-powered torque remains a stubbornly unsolved problem. Meanwhile, theorists and competitors are questioning \"brew ratio bloat\" on the World Brewers Cup stage and experimenting with repurposing spent coffee pucks as secondary filters. It is a relentless drive to extract every ounce of value and flavor from the bean. But true innovation isn't always about overcoming high-tech barriers; sometimes it’s about accessibility, as the Kingrinder P2 recently proved by delivering astonishing pour-over clarity for under $50.","Ultimately, all these threads—the gear, the shifting competition standards, the personal legacies—must tether back to the soil. This was beautifully illustrated in East Java at GCAG Indonesia 2026, where three days of collaborative education brought a global community’s support directly to local producers. As we obsess over torque, taste descriptors, and the science of extraction, it is these moments of shared knowledge at origin that ensure our collective legacy is one worth leaving behind."]},{"id":"ed_2026-10-04","publishDate":"2026-10-04","status":"published","title":"Precision, Prestige, and the Perfect Pour","dek":"As the seasons shift, we explore record-breaking auctions, the physics of kettle streams, and the hidden gems of the global coffee belt.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://perfectdailygrind.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Guatemalan-Coffees.webp","heroCredit":"Coffee News Recap, 2 Oct: Speaker applications open for PRF Mexico, 2026 Best of Panama auction results announced & other stories","heroSource":"Perfect Daily Grind","heroSourceUrl":"https://perfectdailygrind.com/2026/10/coffee-news-recap-2-october-2026/","wordCount":319,"paragraphs":["It’s officially “two hands on a mug” season, and the global coffee industry is warming up right alongside our daily brews. The specialty market continues to prove its resilience and premium appeal, highlighted this week by the Specialty Coffee Association of Panama’s Best of Panama auction, which generated a staggering $3 million in sales. The community is also gearing up for wider connection, with speaker applications opening for Producer & Roaster Forum Mexico and the Belfast Coffee Festival kicking off this week.","But enjoying that perfect autumn cup means wrestling with the unforgiving physics of extraction. For the dedicated home brewer, recent scientific deep dives into pour-over dynamics offer fresh variables to tinker with. Analysts are re-evaluating the physics of kettle streams—specifically how pour rate and height dictate extraction evenness—and exploring the perennial, frustrating question of why we can’t push our pour-over grinds finer without choking the slurry.","Navigating these micro-adjustments requires reliable gear. A new three-year retrospective on the beloved 1Zpresso K-Ultra manual grinder demonstrates how our relationship with precision hand tools evolves as we master them. On the opposite end of the effort spectrum, the premium Jura Z10 is turning heads, asking if a high-end super-automatic can genuinely deliver a zero-compromise specialty experience across its 32 drink options.","Finally, our search for exceptional flavor continues to pull us toward less celebrated, yet deeply fascinating, origins. The Fourth Wave ethos is built on continuous discovery. Take Taiwan: a densely populated island with a century-old, albeit small-scale, coffee-producing history quietly thriving in its mountainous terrain. Halfway across the world, Bolivia sits as the 38th largest producer globally. Yet, it boasts the pristine, high-altitude growing conditions necessary for world-class lots, even if it hasn't historically shared the limelight with its South American neighbors. As we dial in our seasonal recipes, it’s the perfect time to look beyond the usual single-origin suspects and taste what these unique terroirs have to offer."]},{"id":"ed_2026-10-02","publishDate":"2026-10-02","status":"published","title":"The Beautiful Paradox of Modern Coffee","dek":"From adaptive espresso profiles to historical terroirs, the fourth wave continues to balance ruthless precision with profound humanity.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://coffeegeek.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/Mina-Greyscale-1-800x533.jpg","heroCredit":"The Olympia Express Mina: A Beautiful, Frustrating Swiss Paradox","heroSource":"CoffeeGeek","heroSourceUrl":"https://coffeegeek.com/opinions/coffee-at-the-moment/olympia-mina-beautiful-flawed-paradox/","wordCount":303,"paragraphs":["The specialty coffee landscape has always been a tug-of-war between art and rigorous science. This week, that dichotomy is on full display. On the extraction frontier, we are seeing deeper dives into the literal physics of brewing. Recent experiments on puck resistance reveal that deep WDT (Weiss Distribution Technique) significantly improves shot repeatability, while algorithmic innovations on the Decent espresso machine are pioneering profiles that automatically adapt to micro-variations in grind size.","Yet, the pursuit of mechanical perfection is rarely without friction. Take the heavily discussed Olympia Express Mina: a machine that currently serves as a beautiful, frustrating Swiss paradox. Its workflow quirks remind us that sheer aesthetics cannot always compensate for daily reliability—a lesson Canadian home baristas might want to keep in mind as they hunt for impending Black Friday gear upgrades.","Beyond portafilters and pressure profiles, the wider industry continues to elevate everyday accessibility. Look Alive Coffee in St. Petersburg, Florida, is turning heads with its striking new packaging design, proving that a roaster's visual identity remains a vital part of the ritual. Concurrently, Arizona’s Cartel Roasting Co. just snagged two Silver Medals at the Global Coffee Awards, demonstrating that the Ready-to-Drink cold brew category can genuinely rival traditional, made-to-order cafe quality.","Ultimately, however, our futuristic tools and sleek aluminum cans mean nothing without the soils and societies that cultivate the cherry. We continue to look to the origins that anchor our cups. Haiti’s rich, complex history as the world’s first Black-led republic is deeply intertwined with its legacy of coffee production. Half a world away, South Sudan—the globe's youngest nation—is slowly building its own coffee sector, flanked by East African producing giants.","Whether we are meticulously raking espresso grounds with fine needles or exploring emerging agricultural sectors in South Sudan, coffee remains our most beautifully flawed, endlessly fascinating global ritual."]},{"id":"ed_2026-10-01","publishDate":"2026-10-01","status":"published","title":"Beyond the Extraction Threshold: Engineering the Next Era of Specialty Coffee","dek":"On fluid dynamics, institutional capital, and the shifting geography of value.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/phil-sebastian-booc-1.jpg","heroCredit":"Sprudge","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/build-outs-of-coffee-phil-sebastian-in-calgary-alberta-1386731.html","wordCount":268,"paragraphs":["The specialty coffee paradigm is undergoing an essential maturation. What began as a cultural crusade for bean origin and artisan roasting has evolved into a disciplined convergence of applied physics, industrial capital, and geopolitical supply re-alignment. We are no longer simply brewing coffee; we are precision-engineering an ecosystem.","At the bar, extraction science has moved from empirical intuition to fluid mechanics. Micro-interventions—such as analyzing bottom-filter hydraulic resistance or optimizing bed percolation physics—demonstrate how granular control over flow dynamics redefines yield and clarity. This obsession with technical friction extends directly to hardware design. Gear like the Femobook A2 blurs the lines between manual tactile feedback and electric automation, while early seasonal retail cycles push sophisticated gear like the xBloom Studio into broader commercial adoption.","Yet, technical precision in the cup means little without structural durability at origin. Devoción’s newly launched Impact Fund—directing 1% of retail sales toward Colombian farming communities, youth, and ecological restoration—models how roasters must internalize ESG responsibilities directly into transactional unit economics.","Simultaneously, the global supply map is expanding beyond legacy corridors. In West Africa, Côte d'Ivoire continues to leverage its robust agricultural framework to stabilize regional yield dynamics. Across the Arabian Peninsula, Saudi Arabia is translating centuries of deep-rooted coffee culture into strategic domestic production investments—proving that emerging origins are redefining local value retention alongside traditional export giants.","As flagship retail developments, like Phil & Sebastian’s new footprint in Calgary, continue to elevate the physical customer experience, the core directive for industry leadership is clear: real value in the Fourth Wave is created at the intersection of rigorous science, equitable origin economics, and uncompromising execution."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-28","publishDate":"2026-09-28","status":"published","title":"Beyond the Shot: Relentless Curiosity Drives the Next Era of Coffee","dek":"Pursuit of the perfect cup has never been broader","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/madcap-ferndale-1.jpg","heroCredit":"Sprudge","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/build-outs-of-coffee-madcap-coffee-in-grand-rapids-mi-1313815.html","wordCount":284,"paragraphs":["The specialty coffee industry is fundamentally defined by an ethos of relentless curiosity. As we pour over Coffee Review’s newly released Top 50 Coffees of 2025, we are reminded that excellence is not a static achievement, but a moving target. Today, the quest for the perfect cup continues to push radical boundaries in both the café and the laboratory.","Take the latest boundary-pushing inquiries from Barista Hustle. The drive to modernize the iced latte has sparked a reinvention of cold foam, pulling it out of its stagnant 1980s aesthetic to explore genuine iced latte art. Meanwhile, the pursuit of flawless extraction has birthed the \"Wet Weiss\" technique—an experimental collaboration with industrial scientist Professor Abbott that explores adding water prior to tamping to maximize puck density. This high-level experimentation is delightfully democratizing, especially when paired with equipment revelations like the MHW-3Bomber R3 Blade, a grinder that Coffee Chronicler recently exposed as an absolute \"budget beast.\"","That same innovative spirit continues to shape our physical spaces. Sprudge recently highlighted two stunning new café build-outs—Madcap Coffee in Grand Rapids, Michigan, and Cup to Cup Cafe in Savannah, Georgia—proving that the brick-and-mortar experience remains a vital canvas for community and architectural design.","But perhaps the most exciting frontier lies at origin. As roasters hunt for the next exceptional profile, fresh attention is turning toward West Africa. Perfect Daily Grind has recently shined a spotlight on the burgeoning specialty potential in Togo and Liberia. Liberia, Africa’s oldest republic, alongside Togo's narrow coastal strip, are actively proving their capacity to become critical players in the global specialty landscape.","Whether we are hydrating espresso beds or exploring untapped terroirs, the fourth wave of coffee is here—and it refuses to stand still."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-27","publishDate":"2026-09-27","status":"published","title":"Brewing with Precision","dek":"How material science and extraction metrics are democratizing the perfect cup.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/madcap-eureka-sprudge-roasters-village.jpg-copy.jpg","heroCredit":"Sprudge","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/the-best-part-of-waking-up-is-when-september-ends-1367324.html","wordCount":287,"paragraphs":["The specialty coffee industry is continually redefining the boundaries of what is mathematically and chemically possible in a morning cup. At the bleeding edge of this daily innovation is material science, where scientists have recently developed a new graphene oxide coffee filter. This breakthrough technology successfully removes half of the caffeine from a brew while intentionally allowing the other crucial flavor compounds to pass through. It is a massive leap forward for consumers who are seeking robust flavor profiles without the full physiological jolt of standard caffeine levels.","Alongside chemical filtration, mechanical brewing techniques are constantly being refined by experts to squeeze every drop of potential from our beans. Coffee scientists are demonstrating how accessible brewers like the AeroPress can be actively used to reach fuller flavor profiles that are remarkably similar to professional cuppings and high-EY (extraction yield) pour overs. For the truly data-driven barista, this obsession with exact precision extends to the espresso machine. Deep-dive experiments are currently exploring whether the noticeable difference in gravimetric versus volumetric flow could be due to changing shot Total Dissolved Solids (TDS).","As September comes to an end, the broader industry is celebrating the shift in seasons, highlighting weekly curated selections of coffees specifically sourced from the Roaster's Village. Fortunately, you do not need a commercial laboratory to engage with this rapidly evolving coffee landscape. The specialized tools required to chase these high-end, scientific extractions are becoming increasingly accessible to the everyday consumer. Looking ahead to the upcoming holidays, newly released 2025 curated gift lists are already showcasing highly unique and useful espresso and coffee gear. With these curated items priced entirely under $50, outfitting the discerning coffee lover in your life with high-quality gear has never been easier."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-26","publishDate":"2026-09-26","status":"published","title":"Deconstructing the Bean","dek":"Science, Resurgent Terroirs, and the Post-Dogma Era of Coffee","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://www.baristahustle.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/Exploding-Coffee-Bean-03.jpg","heroCredit":"The End of First Crack?","heroSource":"Barista Hustle","heroSourceUrl":"https://www.baristahustle.com/the-end-of-first-crack/","wordCount":334,"paragraphs":["As sensory research challenges long-held roasting tenets, the Fourth Wave looks to emerging origins and high-precision home extraction to redefine the perfect cup.","The transition from Third to Fourth Wave coffee is defined by rigorous scientific inquiry quietly replacing artisanal folklore. This week, we are witnessing a profound deconstruction of foundational coffee dogmas. Groundbreaking discourse is challenging the sacred status of the \"first crack\"—long heralded as the critical, make-or-break pivot point in roast profiling—suggesting our obsessive micro-adjustments during this phase may be far less consequential than previously believed. Furthermore, our very understanding of sensory perception is being upended. Recent research indicates that the organic acids we so confidently claim to taste in a bright, complex pour-over are present in concentrations so low they border on the undetectable. We are no longer just brewing coffee; we are beginning to decode the cognitive illusions of flavor.","This scientific awakening coincides with a necessary geographic expansion of the specialty coffee map. As climate realities shift, the industry is looking beyond traditional equatorial strongholds. We are tracking the compelling resurgence of Sri Lanka—once a global coffee titan in the 1860s before being decimated by leaf rust—now strategically positioning itself to reclaim its heritage through meticulously processed specialty lots. Concurrently, the high-altitude, untapped microclimates of Nepal are drawing serious attention, promising entirely new terroirs and flavor profiles for the discerning palate.","Translating these macro-shifts from the farm and the laboratory to the cup requires both culinary innovation and precise equipment. On the hospitality front, institutions like The Caffè by Mr. Espresso are actively blurring the lines between gastronomy and cafe culture with highly curated seasonal pop-ups. Meanwhile, the democratization of precision extraction continues at home, with newly minted gear guides highlighting the increasingly sophisticated espresso technology now available for under $250.","Ultimately, the Fourth Wave is characterized by this relentless pursuit of clarity. By stripping away outdated sensory myths and expanding our agronomic borders, we continue to elevate coffee from a romanticized craft to a precise, relentlessly evolving science."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-24","publishDate":"2026-09-24","status":"published","title":"Shifts at the Source and the Pursuit of Extraction Perfection","dek":"","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/laila-the-floor.jpg","heroCredit":"Sprudge","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/another-barista-champion-just-competed-in-a-nationally-televised-game-show-1360538.html","wordCount":329,"paragraphs":["As one LA roaster radically rethinks the supply chain, we explore Nepal’s rising terroir, the extremes of bean-by-bean grinding, and a barista champion’s national TV debut.","The structural norms of specialty coffee's supply chain are undergoing significant shifts. Los Angeles specialty coffee company Go Get Em Tiger has launched a \"Roasted At Origin\" initiative, with plans to move 20% of its roasting operations directly to producing countries by the end of 2026, scaling to an ambitious 70% by the end of 2027. This push to retain value at the source parallels the continuous expansion of the global coffee map, highlighting compelling new origins like Nepal. While the landlocked nation lacks the established recognition of Colombia or Ethiopia, its rapid evolution and unique terroir make it an increasingly vital player in the specialty industry.","On the bar, the technical pursuit of the perfect cup continues—sometimes bordering on the absurd. A recent dive by Barista Hustle explored grinding coffee bean-by-bean, categorizing it alongside cryogenic grinding as a form of \"impractical perfection\" that is generally dismissed as inappropriate for commercial environments. A more practical pursuit of perfection can be found in the newest wave of high-extraction espresso filter baskets, where manufacturers have completely re-thought bottom hole patterns to ensure even extraction.","Manual brewing and travel gear are also seeing clever innovations. CoffeeGeek recently detailed a hack to build a fantastic, full-immersion, no-bypass brewer using two inexpensive Hario devices—the Mugen and the Switch. For brewers on the go, the new Outin Mino travel espresso maker promises a smaller footprint, though The Coffee Chronicler notes it represents two steps forward, but one step back where it really matters.","In community news, the organization Not a Barista is testing a new Shopify storefront to provide a smoother shopping experience and clearer product information for its supporters. Finally, specialty coffee continues its crossover into mainstream pop culture, with US Barista Champion Laila Ghambari recently appearing as a contestant on the nationally televised game show The Floor."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-23","publishDate":"2026-09-23","status":"published","title":"From Puck Prep to People Power","dek":"Today’s dispatch highlights the relentless science of extraction, the rise of capable travel espresso, and why the future of the industry belongs to its communities.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/tdg_bellwoodgrantpark_web-@grayson-guldenschuh-1.jpg","heroCredit":"Sprudge","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/build-outs-of-coffee-bellwood-coffee-in-atlanta-ga-2-1327933.html","wordCount":305,"paragraphs":["Specialty coffee has always been a beautiful tug-of-war between precise science and profound human connection. Today’s news perfectly captures that duality, proving that our industry is rapidly evolving on both fronts.","On the human side, the major headline comes from Cafe Imports, which has officially transitioned to a 100% employee-owned model via an ESOP. It’s a massive step forward for equitable business structures in the green coffee sector. Meanwhile, Not a Barista brings us the inspiring story of Bryant Toh, a social worker in Singapore brilliantly utilizing coffee as a bridge to build trust and support youth mental health. It’s a powerful reminder that cafes—like the stunning new Bellwood Coffee build-out in Atlanta recently spotlighted by Sprudge—are more than just retail spaces; they are vital community hubs. The Not a Barista team is also currently on the move, reporting from Dehua in the mountainous Fujian province, tracing the global threads that tie this community together at origin.","Back at the bar, the pursuit of extraction perfection continues unabated. Barista Hustle is digging deep into the physics of puck prep this week. They’re exploring how densification creates more spherical coffee particles for exceptionally even water flow, while also breaking down the ongoing mechanics of the RDT (Ross Droplet Technique) versus the AutoComb. It seems academic science is finally catching up to what home baristas have intuitively known about static reduction for two decades.","For those who refuse to leave this precision at home, The Coffee Chronicler takes a look at the booming travel espresso market. With thorough reviews of the flagship Mhw-3bomber Xpress-58 and the surprisingly capable MiiCoffee Eon (boasting a genuine 51mm basket), battery-operated espresso is proving it’s no longer a gimmick. Whether you're pulling shots in a high-end cafe, a youth center, or out of a backpack, the fourth wave continues to push boundaries."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-22","publishDate":"2026-09-22","status":"published","title":"Shaking the Grounds","dek":"Science, Stance, and Sanctuary","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/cascara-cabinet-rtd-01-1.jpg","heroCredit":"Sprudge","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/ready-to-drink-the-cascara-fizz-boom-from-cascara-cabinet-1278625.html","wordCount":328,"paragraphs":["As extraction methods evolve and unexpected machines earn top marks, the coffee industry is finding its voice on global politics and celebrating the human stories behind the bar.","The specialty coffee community can no longer afford to exist in a vacuum. This reality is brought to the forefront today by CoffeeGeek’s impassioned editorial, which urges coffee professionals and enthusiasts alike to take a definitive stand against fascist policies reshaping our world. Coffee has always been inherently political, and as institutions like Ritual Coffee Roasters unveil their latest stunning build-out in San Francisco, we are reminded that our cafes are the very community hubs where these vital societal conversations take root.","On the bar, we are witnessing a rigorous, joyful re-examination of our mechanics. Barista Hustle explores Lance Hedrick’s recent revelation that simply shaking espresso grounds can dramatically boost extraction, challenging long-held distribution dogmas. They also delve into the potent legacy of the coffee overdose—the famed triple ristretto—with 2024 World Barista Champion Mikael Jasin. Even our gear expectations are being delightfully upended. CoffeeGeek's review of the $549 Ninja Luxe Café Premier proves that what some initially dismissed as a consumer toy is actually an 88.5-scoring powerhouse, proving innovation is becoming more accessible. And for those seeking a lighter refreshment amidst the heavy science, Cascara Cabinet is sparking a fizzy RTD cascara renaissance.","Yet, beneath the high-extraction science and political discourse, coffee remains intimately human. Not a Barista captures this ethos perfectly in two new profiles. They trace Lonika Tay’s inspiring ascent from weighing beans and sealing bags in an Indonesian roastery to commanding the international competition stage. We also get a glimpse into the beautiful chaos of Angel Kotsifakis’s coffee lab, where the intense focus of brewing is punctuated by the gleeful shouts of his child. Seeing a small bicycle nestled among gleaming espresso equipment is a poignant reminder: the Fourth Wave isn’t just about the perfect cup—it’s about honoring the real, complex lives of the people pouring them."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-21","publishDate":"2026-09-21","status":"published","title":"The Machine and the Bean","dek":"Where Coffee's Next Wave Is Breaking","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://coffeechronicler.b-cdn.net/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/Screenshot-2025-04-22-at-16.39.33.jpg","heroCredit":"Sour Espresso: Specialty Coffee’s Uncomfortable Truth","heroSource":"Coffee Chronicler","heroSourceUrl":"https://coffeechronicler.com/why-is-my-espresso-sour/","wordCount":292,"paragraphs":["Coffee has always lived between two impulses—the human hand and the engineered system—and this week the tension is everywhere you look.","On the industry side, the machines are quietly taking over the back office. RoastConsole now offers to run an entire small roastery on a single platform, from roast log to invoice, priced by roasted volume with no add-ons. Smart Care, meanwhile, is rethinking how speed ovens and coffee equipment get serviced, betting on flat rates and faster turnaround. The plumbing of the trade is being rebuilt, and it favors those who can standardize.","But standardization has a shadow. From the origin side comes the harder story. Riza Zahrul Huda's biggest challenge in coffee turned out not to be pests or weather or quality, but something more structural—the quiet difficulty of getting a small village's harvest to a market that will value it. And CoffeeGeek's \"Hollow Harvest\" names the pressure directly: fifty-percent tariffs, a creeping Robusta substitution, and a favorite Brazilian suddenly tasting woody and costing seven dollars more. The cup in your hand is a ledger of forces most drinkers never see.","Which is why the craft still matters. The Coffee Chronicler reminds us that dialing in isn't a cure-all—persistent sourness in espresso points to deeper problems no grind adjustment will fix. Coffee Ad Astra keeps chasing the physics, restraining flow to tame channeling with something as humble as a pasta-strainer mesh. And in Shenzhen, the team behind MOMENTEM spent January doing nothing but testing a grinder, refusing to rush, insisting it behave exactly as they believed it should.","That is the Fourth Wave in miniature: precision engineering in service of an agricultural act, systems and soil pulling against each other—and the best of us refusing to let go of either."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-20","publishDate":"2026-09-20","status":"published","title":"The first lot from Vietnam","dek":"Thirty years. Screening that still has to happen.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://dailycoffeenews.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Grounds-for-Health-auction.png","heroCredit":"Grounds for Health auction. Daily Coffee News.","heroSource":"Daily Coffee News","heroSourceUrl":"https://dailycoffeenews.com/2026/09/16/grounds-for-health-marks-30-years-with-annual-coffee-auction/","wordCount":294,"paragraphs":["Grounds for Health turns thirty this year. The programs sit in Ethiopia and Kenya. More than 265,000 women have been screened for cervical cancer. More than 22,000 have had preventive treatment. The green coffee auction that funds that work opens Wednesday. Crop to Cup sent the first Vietnam lot the auction has ever listed. Great Cherry, in Dak Lak. An anaerobic natural robusta. One hundred thirty two pounds. Cupped at 86. Opening at 5.75 a pound. Justin Mool says the auction is how the industry keeps the connection going.","On Sunday, September 20, 2026, we hold that first lot to one pressure: whether the cup still pays for care at origin when a new room in San Francisco imports Europe, and when the drinker is still a guess.","SPRO Coffee Lab opened at 201 Spear Street, a few blocks from the Ferry Building. European style. Coffees from Barcelona, Copenhagen, Amsterdam, Paris. Modbar. A Weber EG-1 for frozen coffee. Liza Otanes, with her husband, also built The Wild Fox. Japanese roasting partners. A Cup of Excellence Brazil at 105 dollars a cup. Cheryl Hung and Brian Gaffney launched Furthers and Betters because the industry knows the product and guesses at the person who drinks it. After the 7.4 earthquake near San José del Palmar, Jimmy Butler's Bigface and J Balvin put a 78 dollar bundle on El Capitán, net to Colombia Se Levanta. Houthis took Mokha, the port that named mocha. La Cimbali's Supera is rated for 350 cups a day. Sprudge's Roaster's Village would pick them all.","A first lot from Vietnam. Thirty years of screens. The fourth wave is not the auction catalog. It is whether the cup still pays for the woman at origin, and whether the room still names the farm."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-19","publishDate":"2026-09-19","status":"published","title":"The Craft and the Convenience","dek":"","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/green-room-sprudge-lede.jpg","heroCredit":"Sprudge","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/checking-in-on-the-other-side-of-the-supply-chain-with-the-green-room-1349454.html","wordCount":299,"paragraphs":["There's a quiet tension running through coffee right now, and this week's stories draw it out from four directions.","On one side sits convenience. App ordering and drive-thrus are reshaping how most people actually buy their coffee, and the shift is a double-edged sword: faster service and frictionless payment on one hand, the slow erosion of the counter conversation on the other. When the transaction disappears into a phone, something of the ritual goes with it.","On the other side sits craft, and it's alive and well among the people who care about extraction down to the compound. The obsessives are still asking hard questions — how astringency actually gets pulled from the bean, how to prep an espresso puck for a more even shot. This is coffee as a problem worth solving, not just a drink to grab. The gear reflects it too: the Flair 58 remains a serious manual machine years on, wired up with thermocouples to prove it, while newer gadgets like the Outin Claro learn that a clever form factor isn't the same as a useful tool.","Zoom out and the human supply chain comes into focus. A look behind the green-coffee curtain reminds us how many hands touch a cup before we do, and voices from origin — a barista's journey out of lockdown in Malaysia, remote internships built around sustainability and community — insist that coffee's future is a shared, global project, not just a Western café aesthetic.","That's the throughline. The frictionless future is coming fast, and it's genuinely convenient. But the craft, the science, and the people who grow and pour it are what keep coffee worth caring about. The best cafés won't choose between the two. They'll find a way to serve the app order and still remember your name."],"body":"There's a quiet tension running through coffee right now, and this week's stories draw it out from four directions.\n\nOn one side sits convenience. App ordering and drive-thrus are reshaping how most people actually buy their coffee, and the shift is a double-edged sword: faster service and frictionless payment on one hand, the slow erosion of the counter conversation on the other. When the transaction disappears into a phone, something of the ritual goes with it.\n\nOn the other side sits craft, and it's alive and well among the people who care about extraction down to the compound. The obsessives are still asking hard questions — how astringency actually gets pulled from the bean, how to prep an espresso puck for a more even shot. This is coffee as a problem worth solving, not just a drink to grab. The gear reflects it too: the Flair 58 remains a serious manual machine years on, wired up with thermocouples to prove it, while newer gadgets like the Outin Claro learn that a clever form factor isn't the same as a useful tool.\n\nZoom out and the human supply chain comes into focus. A look behind the green-coffee curtain reminds us how many hands touch a cup before we do, and voices from origin — a barista's journey out of lockdown in Malaysia, remote internships built around sustainability and community — insist that coffee's future is a shared, global project, not just a Western café aesthetic.\n\nThat's the throughline. The frictionless future is coming fast, and it's genuinely convenient. But the craft, the science, and the people who grow and pour it are what keep coffee worth caring about. The best cafés won't choose between the two. They'll find a way to serve the app order and still remember your name."},{"id":"ed_2026-09-18","publishDate":"2026-09-18","status":"published","title":"He pointed at the board","dek":"Fourteen. A pour-over instead.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://notabarista.b-cdn.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Jimmy-Adame-1024x576.jpg","heroCredit":"Jimmy Adame. Not a Barista.","heroSource":"Not a Barista","heroSourceUrl":"https://notabarista.org/jimmy-adame/","wordCount":281,"paragraphs":["A 14-year-old kid at a Michigan farmer's market. Flannel and Neff beanies. He points at the board. He thinks he is ordering a blackberry-milk-chocolate latte. The barista hands him a pour-over instead. Jimmy Adame. Not a Barista tells the rest as coffee, chaos, and second acts. The board had a name. The cup had a farm. He stayed long enough for the second act to start.","On Friday, September 18, 2026, we hold that pour-over to one pressure: whether the cup still arrives as what the farm grew, not as the name on the board, when a third group is already scrap, and when a label says direct.","Stumptown is in Los Angeles. A flagship in the historic Lincoln Heights Theatre. Motors Coffee of Paris takes over Sey in Bushwick this weekend. Paris in Brooklyn for a few days. Barista Hustle says the resale on a one-group high-end machine is staggering. A three-group is terrible. The industry does not value the third group. A second-hand Linea opened first cafes. The MVP is newer and costs more. Coffee Chronicler put the Kingrinder K7 on the bench. It tries a lot. It almost succeeds. Except where it is most crucial. CoffeeGeek asks what direct trade means for the farmer who grew the cup. Direct. Fair. Commodity. Three labels. Three promises. They are not the same. Edward Choo did not grow up loving coffee. In Malaysia the drink was Milo. At the kopitiam he stole a sip. Bitter. Intense. Not something he wanted.","A pour-over instead of the board. Milo first. Then a sip. The fourth wave is not the third group. It is whether someone still hands you the cup the farm made."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-17","publishDate":"2026-09-17","status":"published","title":"The baristas at the center","dek":"Helen Diller. A long curved bar.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/sightglass-berkeley-4.jpg","heroCredit":"Sightglass Coffee, Berkeley. Sprudge.","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/build-outs-of-coffee-sightglass-coffee-in-berkeley-ca-1275259.html","wordCount":283,"paragraphs":["Sightglass opened its fifth cafe on May 8, 2026, on the ground floor of UC Berkeley's Helen Diller Anchor House, where downtown meets campus. Lundberg Design started from filtration and percolation, and from a vintage Probat. Concentric circles from the logo organize the plan. A long curved bar puts the baristas, and the work of making coffee, at the center of the room. Overhead, a soffit takes the shape of a bean hopper. A drum of steel mesh filters the daylight.","On Thursday, September 17, 2026, we hold that bar to one pressure: whether the work of making coffee is still allowed to sit in the middle of the room when the cone is called out of control, and when decaf costs the same as the good stuff.","The Global Coffee Awards Latin America edition, 12 August, named Café Baluarte de Veracruz overall. Producers sit on the same card. Professor Steven Abbott calls the filter cone crazy, an out of control system. The UX method tries to separate the variables with a lid and a magnetic stirrer. CoffeeGeek says decaf now costs as much as the good stuff. A paint-stripper headline. Descafecol in Manizales ran out of slots. Green from Colombia sailed to Germany and back. The Flair 49 Pro is a passive lever on the 49 millimeter puck. The sample basket had holes off center. Barista Hustle: no stickers on the espresso machine. Jane Cabusas Espante still chose the smaller cezve stage. Not a Barista, quiet from Shanghai toward Java. Young farmers. What comes next.","A curved bar. Baristas in the middle. The fourth wave is not a louder championship. It is whether the work still has the center of the room."],"body":"Sightglass opened its fifth cafe on May 8, 2026, on the ground floor of UC Berkeley's Helen Diller Anchor House, where downtown meets campus. Lundberg Design started from filtration and percolation, and from a vintage Probat. Concentric circles from the logo organize the plan. A long curved bar puts the baristas, and the work of making coffee, at the center of the room. Overhead, a soffit takes the shape of a bean hopper. A drum of steel mesh filters the daylight.\n\nOn Thursday, September 17, 2026, we hold that bar to one pressure: whether the work of making coffee is still allowed to sit in the middle of the room when the cone is called out of control, and when decaf costs the same as the good stuff.\n\nThe Global Coffee Awards Latin America edition, 12 August, named Café Baluarte de Veracruz overall. Producers sit on the same card. Professor Steven Abbott calls the filter cone crazy, an out of control system. The UX method tries to separate the variables with a lid and a magnetic stirrer. CoffeeGeek says decaf now costs as much as the good stuff. A paint-stripper headline. Descafecol in Manizales ran out of slots. Green from Colombia sailed to Germany and back. The Flair 49 Pro is a passive lever on the 49 millimeter puck. The sample basket had holes off center. Barista Hustle: no stickers on the espresso machine. Jane Cabusas Espante still chose the smaller cezve stage. Not a Barista, quiet from Shanghai toward Java. Young farmers. What comes next.\n\nA curved bar. Baristas in the middle. The fourth wave is not a louder championship. It is whether the work still has the center of the room."},{"id":"ed_2026-09-16","publishDate":"2026-09-16","status":"published","title":"She chose the smaller stage","dek":"A cezve that still counts. Work at the center of the room.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://notabarista.b-cdn.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Jane-1-scaled.jpg","heroCredit":"Jane Cabusas Espante. Not a Barista.","heroSource":"Not a Barista","heroSourceUrl":"https://notabarista.org/%f0%9d%96%a9%f0%9d%96%ba%f0%9d%97%87%f0%9d%96%be-%f0%9d%96%a2%f0%9d%96%ba%f0%9d%96%bb%f0%9d%97%8e%f0%9d%97%8c%f0%9d%96%ba%f0%9d%97%8c-%f0%9d%96%a4%f0%9d%97%8c%f0%9d%97%89%f0%9d%96%ba%f0%9d%97%87/","wordCount":319,"paragraphs":["Jane Cabusas Espante chose the smaller stage. Cezve. Ibrik. Next to the Barista Championship or Brewers Cup, the pot rarely draws the same room. The return is uncertain. Sometimes the contest does not happen at all. She went anyway. The traditional brew. A story. A line back to the root. In Dubai she won the 2026 World Cezve/Ibrik Championship for the United Arab Emirates. A carbonic macerated Colombia Gesha with a natural Panama Gesha. Cryoconcentrate. Strawberries. Angel Kotsifakis second. Wael Hachem third. Not a Barista says the return was not measured in exposure.","On Wednesday, September 16, 2026, we hold that smaller stage to one pressure: whether the oldest brew still has a room when the cone is called out of control, and when a new cafe puts the work in the middle.","Sightglass opened its fifth cafe on the ground floor of UC Berkeley's Helen Diller Anchor House. Lundberg Design started from filtration and a vintage Probat. A long curved bar. The baristas at the center. The Global Coffee Awards Latin America edition, 12 August, named the region's best roasters and the producers behind those bags. Professor Steven Abbott calls the filter cone crazy. An out of control system. Their UX method tries to separate the variables. A magnetic stirrer. CoffeeGeek says decaf now costs as much as the good stuff. A paint stripper ban. Descafecol in Manizales ran out of slots. Green from Colombia sailed to Germany and back. The Flair 49 Pro is a passive lever on the 49 millimeter puck. The sample basket had holes off center. Barista Hustle says do not put stickers on the espresso machine. I'M NOT A BARISTA has been quiet from Shanghai toward Java. Young farmers. What comes next.","A smaller stage. Work at the center of the room. The fourth wave is not the louder championship. It is whether someone still chooses the pot that has to be kept."],"body":"Jane Cabusas Espante chose the smaller stage. Cezve. Ibrik. Next to the Barista Championship or Brewers Cup, the pot rarely draws the same room. The return is uncertain. Sometimes the contest does not happen at all. She went anyway. The traditional brew. A story. A line back to the root. In Dubai she won the 2026 World Cezve/Ibrik Championship for the United Arab Emirates. A carbonic macerated Colombia Gesha with a natural Panama Gesha. Cryoconcentrate. Strawberries. Angel Kotsifakis second. Wael Hachem third. Not a Barista says the return was not measured in exposure.\n\nOn Wednesday, September 16, 2026, we hold that smaller stage to one pressure: whether the oldest brew still has a room when the cone is called out of control, and when a new cafe puts the work in the middle.\n\nSightglass opened its fifth cafe on the ground floor of UC Berkeley's Helen Diller Anchor House. Lundberg Design started from filtration and a vintage Probat. A long curved bar. The baristas at the center. The Global Coffee Awards Latin America edition, 12 August, named the region's best roasters and the producers behind those bags. Professor Steven Abbott calls the filter cone crazy. An out of control system. Their UX method tries to separate the variables. A magnetic stirrer. CoffeeGeek says decaf now costs as much as the good stuff. A paint stripper ban. Descafecol in Manizales ran out of slots. Green from Colombia sailed to Germany and back. The Flair 49 Pro is a passive lever on the 49 millimeter puck. The sample basket had holes off center. Barista Hustle says do not put stickers on the espresso machine. I'M NOT A BARISTA has been quiet from Shanghai toward Java. Young farmers. What comes next.\n\nA smaller stage. Work at the center of the room. The fourth wave is not the louder championship. It is whether someone still chooses the pot that has to be kept."},{"id":"ed_2026-09-15","publishDate":"2026-09-15","status":"published","title":"Named for the world","dek":"Buon Ma Thuot. Farms that held.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/coffee_museum_coffee_harvesting_artifacts_exhibition_area_world_coffee_museum_07.jpg","heroCredit":"World Coffee Museum, Buon Ma Thuot. Sprudge.","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/in-vietnam-the-world-coffee-museum-tells-a-global-story-1250720.html","wordCount":289,"paragraphs":["In Buon Ma Thuot, in Vietnam’s coffee highlands, the World Coffee Museum tells a global story. Not a Vietnam museum. World. Jens Burg spent two decades collecting. More than 10,000 artifacts moved from Hamburg after Dang Le Nguyen Vu of Trung Nguyen acquired the collection. The building takes the longhouse of the Ede. Coffee as shared heritage, from a producing nation.","On Tuesday, September 15, 2026, we hold that naming to one pressure: whether the cup still belongs to the farm when the season is sold in a box, and when a pitcher has to be split so both drinks get the foam.","Puerto Rico was once the world’s seventh largest producer. About 2,000 farms now. Irma and Maria took up to 90 percent of the plants. They held. Jamaica’s Blue Mountain is less than 0.01 percent of world supply. About 80 percent of those farms sit under five acres. Japan still takes most of it. Sprudge says pumpkin spice is dead. Titan Caskets sells an orange box for sixteen hundred dollars. PSL season does not have to end when you do. Barista Hustle teaches how to split a pitcher so both drinks get the same foam. Four quarters. Pour three, return one. Nutation packs the puck denser. Coarse particles fall. Some of those boulders may only have been clumps. CoffeeGeek says the grinder is still the first tool. Twelve budget picks. A Kingrinder at 45 dollars. An Encore that will not retire. Moderate coffee, three to four cups, still looks after the brain and the heart.","A museum named for the world. Farms that held. The fourth wave is not the casket for the season. It is whether the cup still belongs to the farm that grew it."],"body":"In Buon Ma Thuot, in Vietnam’s coffee highlands, the World Coffee Museum tells a global story. Not a Vietnam museum. World. Jens Burg spent two decades collecting. More than 10,000 artifacts moved from Hamburg after Dang Le Nguyen Vu of Trung Nguyen acquired the collection. The building takes the longhouse of the Ede. Coffee as shared heritage, from a producing nation.\n\nOn Tuesday, September 15, 2026, we hold that naming to one pressure: whether the cup still belongs to the farm when the season is sold in a box, and when a pitcher has to be split so both drinks get the foam.\n\nPuerto Rico was once the world’s seventh largest producer. About 2,000 farms now. Irma and Maria took up to 90 percent of the plants. They held. Jamaica’s Blue Mountain is less than 0.01 percent of world supply. About 80 percent of those farms sit under five acres. Japan still takes most of it. Sprudge says pumpkin spice is dead. Titan Caskets sells an orange box for sixteen hundred dollars. PSL season does not have to end when you do. Barista Hustle teaches how to split a pitcher so both drinks get the same foam. Four quarters. Pour three, return one. Nutation packs the puck denser. Coarse particles fall. Some of those boulders may only have been clumps. CoffeeGeek says the grinder is still the first tool. Twelve budget picks. A Kingrinder at 45 dollars. An Encore that will not retire. Moderate coffee, three to four cups, still looks after the brain and the heart.\n\nA museum named for the world. Farms that held. The fourth wave is not the casket for the season. It is whether the cup still belongs to the farm that grew it."},{"id":"ed_2026-09-14","publishDate":"2026-09-14","status":"published","title":"The question stayed","dek":"Cycling before school. Beans falling through a hole in Boise","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/slow-by-slow-11.jpg","heroCredit":"Sprudge","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/build-outs-of-coffee-slow-by-slow-in-boise-id-1274400.html","wordCount":315,"paragraphs":["Kay Cheon’s coffee began with a question that did not go away. He cycled before school. Coffee was just there. Then friends handed him cups that were not merely better. Different. He needed to know why. A V60. A shop in Los Angeles in 2013. The stage in 2018 to learn, not to win. In 2025 he became United States Barista Champion. He says the community is what kept him. Curiosity and flavor would not have been enough. In Santa Barbara he is building Parterre. A garden. Nature, arranged.","On Monday, September 15, 2026, we hold that question to one pressure: whether the cup still answers when the brew is slow on purpose, and when the beans fall through a hole in the bar.","Not a Barista went to Chongqing for ICCF First as friends. The room was packed. Four people stood around one brew, passed it, laughed. Coffee felt close again. Sprudge walked Slow by Slow in Boise. Ten years. Fast by Slow opened next door on July 17. Same coffee, faster. Under the bar a Weber EG-1 eats beans through a hand-carved vortex. You watch them disappear. Accademia del Caffè Espresso in Florence is dropping Coffee Facts. Sixteen topics. Four chapters. Seed to cup. Jonathan Gagné read 300 particle size distributions on 24 espresso grinders. Dialing in is dialing in the fines. He got the Pulsar. A valve. No bypass. A centimeter of slurry. Coffee Review cupped nearly 200 darker roasts. The top ten sat Agtron 44 to 47, the cusp of second crack. Mark Prince put cà phê sữa đá back. A phin. Four to five minutes. Condensed milk is structural. The slow drip is the point.","A question that stayed. A packed room in Chongqing. Beans falling through a hole. The fourth wave is not the chapter count. It is whether someone still stays long enough for the cup to answer."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-13","publishDate":"2026-09-13","status":"published","title":"Already there","dek":"Coffee since six, a new room in Copenhagen.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/8th-roast-kilimbi-anaerobic-sprudge-roasters-village-copy.jpg","heroCredit":"Sprudge","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/a-fresh-roster-of-fresh-coffees-from-the-sprudge-roasters-village-1332178.html","wordCount":293,"paragraphs":["For Andrés Paparoni, coffee was never something he found later. Growing up in Venezuela, it was family. His father, Gustavo, a Q grader and teacher. His grandfather sold Gaggia. He drank it from six. At fourteen he was behind the bar with his father. Not every branch of the family stayed in coffee. Enough of it stayed to shape the room he grew up in. A Gardelli Panama and a Sasa Sestic routine made him want to train, compete, stay. He is now a Spanish barista champion, instructing at Nomad in Barcelona. Origin was not a tasting note. It was the house.","On Sunday, September 13, 2026, we hold that house to one pressure: who the cup is for when the bag is fresh from a village, and when the scale does every job.","Sprudge restocked the Roaster's Village. A weekly list because people keep asking where to buy. Sprudge put Fresh Pots in Copenhagen on the Build-Outs. A new cafe. A new counter. CoffeeGeek put a Laybird MagAttach on the bench. Sixty nine dollars. Magnets. One tiny scale for espresso and pour over, no app, a live ratio in red. Option-O's Lagom Casa is the other all rounder: slow conical burrs, a magnetic cup, a knocker, espresso and filter without a second machine. Jonathan Gagné's Physics of Espresso is out, graphs and drawings, sold through Scott Rao. I'M NOT A BARISTA took charity wristbands, L1GHT cups, and a MOMENTEM grinder to Bangkok. Not only to show the work.","A life that was already coffee. A bag that arrived this week. A room that just opened. The fourth wave is not the scale that does many jobs. It is whether the cup still belongs to the house that had it from the start."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-12","publishDate":"2026-09-12","status":"published","title":"It began with people","dek":"Tay in the first year. A pouring arm on autopilot.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://coffeegeek.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/xBloom-Studio-Hero-1.jpg","heroCredit":"xBloom Studio. CoffeeGeek.","heroSource":"CoffeeGeek","heroSourceUrl":"https://coffeegeek.com/blog/new-products/xbloom-studio-competition-level-pour-over-on-autopilot/","wordCount":302,"paragraphs":["For Tay Wipvasutt, coffee began with people. Still in his first year of university, he took a part time job at a coffee shop. Not a flavor first. Not a machine. Not a scoresheet. People. Simon Sun, as a child, lost a dog that had lived with the family four or five years. What stayed was not only the loss. He never knew what happened. If it had died, he thought, at least he would have known.","On Saturday, September 12, 2026, we hold that first job to one pressure: who the cup is for when the arm pours itself, and when the cezve is faster than the spro.","You are at home. You want coffee. You are already going out the door. Barista Hustle says it is not espresso. The cezve is mightier than the spro when time is the only brief. Another piece splits the bar: some steam one drink at a time, some steam in twos. One is not better. Latte art champions tend to steam for two. Sprudge gathered more gadgets this week. Gearly beloved. Perfect Daily Grind recapped the rest: Global Coffee Awards for US and Canada roasters, a Uganda and South Korea deal, Luckin taking a billion dollar stake story, Joe Coffee and Cometeer with JetBlue. Coffee Chronicler put the cup last: ceramic or glass as the final piece of the flavor puzzle. CoffeeGeek put the xBloom Studio on autopilot. Whole bean to cup. Grinder, scale, kettle, an oscillating pouring arm. Competition level pour over without a hand on the kettle.","A first year behind the bar. A dog that did not come back. An arm that pours. The fourth wave is not the gadget of the week. It is whether the cup still begins with people when the kettle has learned to move by itself."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-11","publishDate":"2026-09-11","status":"published","title":"She walked into the room","dek":"Noon at twenty seven. Extra hot is not extra good.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://notabarista.b-cdn.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/noon-1024x576.jpg","heroCredit":"Noon Nutrada Kunavivattananon. Not a Barista.","heroSource":"Not a Barista","heroSourceUrl":"https://notabarista.org/noon-nutrada-kunavivattananon/","wordCount":285,"paragraphs":["Noon Nutrada Kunavivattananon walked into a room at twenty seven as CEO of one of Thailand's largest specialty coffee companies. A few years later she walked into another as the first female president of the Specialty Coffee Association of Thailand. Not a Barista tells that walk. The same week the studio paused most operations for family. The 135 ml L1GHT cups already packed still went out.","On Friday, September 11, 2026, we hold that walk to one pressure: who still sits with the cup when extra hot is the risk, and when the shot is already in the fridge.","Sprudge says extra hot coffee is not extra good for the throat. Esophageal cancer risk rises with temperature, not with the bean. Extra hot is a temperature, not a roast. Blanchard's Coffee put Ninety Three Million on a bag. Design as the name on the door. Barista Hustle cupped prebatched espresso against fresh. A podcast voice said we should be hyping this more. Another piece counts World Barista Championship time: twelve drinks in fifteen minutes. Seventy five seconds a drink. The title counts two hundred ten. The clock is the routine. Coffee Chronicler put the DeLonghi Essenza Mini on the bench. Heat in twenty five seconds. Width of a hand. Hot shots. Nice crema. Not really espresso. Easy mode. The same desk still ranks drip: Moccamaster, Aiden, Breville Precision. Peak automatic. The question is which one you live with.","A woman in the room. A bag with a number. A shot that waited. The fourth wave is not the machine that heats in half a minute. It is whether someone still walks in and sits down with the cup at a temperature a throat can keep."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-09","publishDate":"2026-09-09","status":"published","title":"Not just the judges","dek":"Champions on the road. Milk that takes on water from the wand","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://perfectdailygrind.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Boram-Um.webp","heroCredit":"Why the world’s best baristas are going on tour","heroSource":"Perfect Daily Grind","heroSourceUrl":"https://perfectdailygrind.com/2026/09/baristas-going-on-tour/","wordCount":295,"paragraphs":["Champion baristas are going on tour. Bar takeovers. Tastings. A walk through the competition routine for people who never sat with the judges. Jack Simpson, 2025 World Barista Champion, is with FAEMA in Asia Pacific. On 14 September he is in Barcelona for a tasting and a live Q and A. Boram Um took the same road after 2023, across North America, Europe, and Asia. The events rarely turn a profit. The title still has to meet the room.","On Friday, September 11, 2026, we hold that room to one pressure: who still pours for the people who drink it when the milk is a bot, and when the crema is made after the shot.","The Global Coffee Awards named Sweet Bloom Coffee of Denver overall winner for the United States and Canada. Houston, 10 and 11 August. Double blind. Q graders. Barista Hustle weighed steamed milk at 55 C and found 7.5 to 10 percent extra water from the wand. Jack Simpson and Anthony Douglas concentrate milk instead. Automated foamers do not add that water. Ubermilk Plus can tap alternative milks. A later blind test of fake crema, a Nanofoamer on premade espresso, went 9 to 1 against the fake. The fake was more acrid. Asser Christensen unpacked a grinder in Denmark and brewed a coffee he knew. It tasted like nothing. Fruit gone. Danish tap. Filter coffee, he says, wants water between 30 and 100 TDS. The Breville Bambino Plus still pulls shots he likes, years on. A beginner machine.","A champion in a room that is not a stage. A wand that waters the milk. A cup that went quiet. The fourth wave is not the trophy. It is whether the pour still happens in front of the people who drink it."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-08","publishDate":"2026-09-08","status":"published","title":"Not a statement cafe","dek":"Two dollars in Over-the-Rhine. A beach already had the brief.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://dailycoffeenews.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Radical-Bean-Cincinatti-Ohio-1.jpg","heroCredit":"Radical Bean Cooperative, Over-the-Rhine. Daily Coffee News.","heroSource":"Daily Coffee News","heroSourceUrl":"https://dailycoffeenews.com/2026/09/08/radical-bean-wants-to-rework-the-coffee-model-in-cincinnati/","wordCount":294,"paragraphs":["Radical Bean Cooperative reopened in the Volkshaus, northern Over-the-Rhine, after Topia shut for two months and nearly stayed shut. Rayan Saoud is the sole worker-owner. Drip is two dollars. Urbana roasts. Lebanese pastry from Dean's Mediterranean. The boutique pourover shop is gone. The neighbors are not.","On Tuesday, September 8, 2026, we hold those two dollars to one pressure: whether the room still belongs to the street that walks past it.","Eventide Coffee opened three thousand square feet near the sea in Chennai. Billboards, the architects, said the brief was never a statement cafe. Calm. The beach already had everything they needed. Ibrahim H. Al Mallouhi left a vice president's chair to chase a smell from a grandmother roasting on the floor near the port. He wants to be remembered for serving the world's best coffee, not the title. Origin in the hand. Then a shop that can hold it without performing.","Barista Hustle watched World Brewers Cup finalists updose to an average 1:14.1. Classic third-wave ratios sat at 1:16 to 1:17. The scoresheet changed. The street did not. The spent puck is still being asked to become a filter for the next shot. Mark Prince put cordless espresso grinders on the bench. USB-C will jump a diesel in winter. It will not, yet, hold torque through a dense light roast. Hand grinding 18.5 grams took forty-five seconds. The motor took longer. A leftover is a fact. A score is a fashion. A battery is a promise.","A cooperative on McMicken. A curved counter facing the Madras beach. A crank that does not sag. The fourth wave is not a new brand. It is whether Cincinnati and Chennai are still in the same sentence: the room belongs as long as they will have us."]},{"id":"ed_2026-09-07","publishDate":"2026-09-07","status":"published","title":"The crank still turns","dek":"A tenth shop in Greenpoint. A motor that stalls on a dense bean.","authorName":"Dr. Wallace Lynch","authorTitle":"Editor in Chief","heroImage":"https://sprudge.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/variety-coffee-1.jpg","heroCredit":"Variety Coffee Roasters, Greenpoint. Sprudge Build-Outs of Coffee.","heroSource":"Sprudge","heroSourceUrl":"https://sprudge.com/build-outs-of-coffee-variety-coffee-roasters-in-brooklyn-ny-1265757.html","wordCount":286,"paragraphs":["Variety Coffee Roasters opened its tenth cafe in Greenpoint, back where the company started. Nearly twenty years. Queens. Manhattan. Still independent. No outside investor. Sprudge walked the new room, and posted this week's Roaster's Village list besides.","On Sunday, September 6, 2026, we hold that room to one pressure: what still turns when you unplug the machine, and who still owns the key.","Ibrahim H. Al Mallouhi left a vice president's chair in telecom to chase a smell he first met as a boy in an old house near the port, his grandmother roasting on the floor. He wants to be remembered for serving the best coffee the world produces. Not the biggest title. He found specialty coffee in a New York cafe called Fika. Then he built The Espresso Lab. The fourth wave is that arc: origin in the hand, then a shop that can hold it.","Barista Hustle asks if World Brewers Cup scoring for taste descriptors is inflating brew ratios — more water, more theater. The spent puck is still being asked to become compost, board, filter. A leftover is a fact. A score is a fashion.","Mark Prince put cordless espresso grinders on the bench. USB-C will jump a diesel in winter. It will not, yet, hold torque through a dense light roast at 300 microns. A screwdriver on a Kingrinder stalls. Hand grinding 18.5 grams took 45 seconds. The motor took longer, then the shot choked on fines. The arm is still the flywheel.","A tenth cafe without a fund. A grandmother's pan. A crank that does not sag. The fourth wave is not a new battery. It is whether Greenpoint and the port house are still in the same sentence."]}];
window.EDITORIAL.body = (window.EDITORIAL.paragraphs || []).join("\n\n");
